/* Copyright 2026 Rhett Creighton - Apache License 2.0
 *
 * extractors.def — the manifest of DERIVATIONS, not of facts.
 *
 * The ontology is a projection. Every row in it is produced by re-reading a
 * registry that already exists and is already authoritative; nothing is
 * authored in the ontology itself. This file is the only checked-in artifact
 * the ontology owns, and it deliberately contains no facts about the system —
 * only a statement of which canonical source feeds which entity kind, through
 * which extractor, emitting which edges.
 *
 * That distinction is what keeps this from becoming a second ledger. A
 * manifest of facts would need a freshness gate, and a freshness gate is the
 * drift-detection mechanism the whole design exists to avoid. A manifest of
 * derivations cannot go stale in that way: the build generates the extractor
 * dispatch table from these rows, so a row naming a function that does not
 * exist fails the link, and a canonical registry that no row claims fails
 * check_onto_source_binding.sh.
 *
 * ── Row contract ──
 *
 *   ONTO_EXTRACTOR(kind, source_glob, extractor_fn, edge_kinds, required_fields)
 *
 *   kind             entity kind this extractor produces, lowercase, singular
 *                    ("command", "dumper", "test_group"). One kind may appear
 *                    in more than one row when it has several canonical
 *                    sources (db_table does — six independent schema owners).
 *   source_glob      ':'-separated repo-relative globs naming the canonical
 *                    source(s). Feeds BOTH the lint gate's coverage check and
 *                    the store's staleness stamp, so it must name every file
 *                    the extractor reads and no file it does not.
 *   extractor_fn     C identifier. Stringized into the generated dispatch
 *                    table; must be a real symbol or the link fails.
 *   edge_kinds       ':'-separated edge kinds this extractor may emit. Every
 *                    name must appear in onto_edges.def. An extractor that
 *                    emits an edge kind not listed here is a store error, not
 *                    a warning — the declaration is the authority.
 *   required_fields  ':'-separated field names that must be non-empty on
 *                    every row of the canonical source. This is what turns
 *                    "the row exists" into "the row carries its edges", and
 *                    it is what check_onto_edge_completeness.sh enforces.
 *
 * Keep each row on ONE logical line: the lint gates parse this file with awk
 * and do not implement C line-continuation.
 *
 * ── Adding a registry ──
 *
 * Do not widen an existing glob to swallow a new registry. A new KIND of
 * canonical source is a decision about what it means and what it connects to,
 * and it should cost one row here saying so. Widening a glob buries that
 * decision and is the specific failure the binding gate refuses.
 */

/* clang-format off */

ONTO_EXTRACTOR("command", "config/commands/*.def", onto_extract_commands,
               "handled_by:emits_schema:allowed_on_lane:requires_cap",
               "path:summary:semantics")

ONTO_EXTRACTOR("dumper", "app/controllers/include/controllers/diagnostics_dumpers.def",
               onto_extract_dumpers,
               "dumped_by:dumper_owns_file:proven_by",
               "name:desc:owner_file:primary_test")

/* clang-format on */
